Facebook CEO Mark Zuckerberg  congressional testimony highlighted the  debate over how much tech company should  protect your data your digital footprint  goes far beyond social media sites like  Facebook cloud fair is a global service  that offers protection from hackers and  faster speeds to more than 7 million  websites it serves more web traffic than  Twitter Amazon Apple Instagram Bing and  Wikipedia combined now cloud fair is out  with a new product aimed at consumers  the company believes this will make it  more difficult for businesses to track  your internet activity CloudFlare it  also says it can speed up your overall  service online Matthew Prince is the CEO  Matthew good morning good morning so  talk about this service that you provide  Clough lers core service is to make the  internet faster and safer for anyone  who's trying to put any kind of content  or service online but what we announced  recently with our new 1.1 1.1 service  which is a lot of ones is that we can  help consumers protect a little bit of  their data from leaking to ISPs and  other businesses that might be tracking  them on the Internet  and we're excited to be providing this  to services and for the first time offer  it to consumers before we break this  down what does one dot one dot one dot  one stand for other than just for ones  in a row well one of the things that's  hard about the internet is that your  computer doesn't know google.com or  amazon.com and so it has to look to a  directory almost like the white pages  for the internet to figure out the  address of where that is and what one  dot one dot one dot one for ones is is  it's a service that provides that  directory and speeds it up for your  actual browsing and make sure that your  your information isn't being tracked  online and one of the takeaways from the  hearings that we saw with Mark  Zuckerberg this week is that there seem  to be a disconnect between Congress and  the tech world right some of the  questions imply that there's not much  understanding as to what these tech  companies do how important is that for  you to relay to users because to  understand being hacked would mean to  understand your service in  all right yeah I think that you know the  the thing which is important for  consumers to understand is that there  are some tech companies that sell  products companies like CloudFlare and  Apple and then there are some tech  companies that actually sell you as the  user and your attention to advertisers  and technology isn't inherently about  compromising privacy or protecting  privacy it really depends on what the  tech tech company's business model is  and CloudFlare our business model is to  make sure the internet is fast and  protected from hackers and scammers and  so it was very aligned with us to say we  don't want to sell our customers data  our users data for advertising because  that's that's just never been a part of  our business model and so it's it's not  easy enough to say that tech is pro or  anti privacy I think you have to look at  an ax company by company basis so what  is the business model how does  CloudFlare make money we service  customers who pay us to use our global  network which spans a hundred and fifty  cities around the world to make the  internet faster and again protect them  from hackers and scammers online and so  everyone from small businesses up to you  know some of the world's largest  companies use our network in order to  ensure that they're fast and that their  services are always available but the  thing is Mathew consumers are of two  minds about privacy too when it comes to  finding a restaurant in their  neighborhood they're irritated when the  app doesn't already anticipate where  they live and can find the restaurants  next to them if it says to them hey will  you tell us where you live so we can  give you the restaurants they say oh  sure and it's all those little tiny  little decisions that then when they're  aggregating people say oh my gosh I've  given away my whole life but in a lot of  ways they've given it because they want  the the the ease of use so isn't that  the what the tension is here in terms of  consumer privacy you know I think what  the hearings showed is that consumers  often don't understand when they're  being tracked online for instance when  we launch 1.1.1 to one people were  surprised to know that not only could  ISPs see every website that you went to  online but actually that Congress  relaxed the rules and allowed them to  then sell that browsing data to  advertisers  and so I think what's really important  is that there's clarity across all of  the tech industry of what data is being  captured and that you give consumers the  ability to actually opt out of that  information if they don't want that  information to be now I'm so glad you  brought that up because I think in sort  of this outrage over privacy every  single web search that we make that  information can be sold right just in a  simple that's right and I think that  consumers don't understand that and we  frame this around privacy but I think  what consumers really want is control  they want to be able to say this is my  data I understand who I'm giving it to  sometimes when I give that data to them  they can put a a service can provide a  better experience to me but I don't want  it to feel it's creepy when you know the  handbag that you are looking at online  or the pair of shoes starts following  you around the internet we've all had  that experience that's right so let me  ask you about regulation then because  it's something that the tech industry  shunned for years up until now right  even heard Mike Mark Zuckerberg say that  he would agree to some that was valuable  regarding the details how do you feel  about the subject matter and what effect  do you think the government could have  an implementing proper regulation you  know I think that regulation is an  important for any industry including the  tech industry but it's important to get  that right and what's tough is whenever  there's new technology that comes along  whether that was the telephone or  television or or now the internet it  takes a while for social norms and  understandings to develop and then once  you have those you can start to put  together regulations so I think that you  know like Mark Zuckerberg like I think  most of the tech industry we don't  welcome sensible regulation that talks  about things like being transparent  about what data is being collected about  you knowing that you can get that data  back from the providers that you that  you offer it to and for us you know  that's easy for us to support because  again that's never been our business  model and so I think that a lot of the  tech industry would actually you know  welcome what was really sensible in like  regulation because we're tech users  ourselves and it creeps me  when my data is flowing across the  internet all right we're gonna have to  leave it there Matthew thank you so much

